        "# Continuity\n",
        "\n",
        "_Continuity_ is a long-standing concept that anyone who spends enough time in mathematics will encounter repeatedly. Back in high school, most of the functions we worked with—single-variable ones like polynomials, exponentials, and trigonometric functions—were continuous, at least on most of their domains. Rational functions such as $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x - a}$ are also continuous almost everywhere, except at points where the expression is not defined, that is, where the denominator becomes zero.\n",
        "\n",
        "In high school, the so-called \"definition\" of continuity is usually introduced in a very pictorial way. A function $f$ is said to be continuous if, when sketching its graph, one can draw it without lifting the pen from the paper.\n",
        "\n",
        "It is a remarkably intuitive idea—almost so intuitive that it feels suspiciously informal for something that later becomes a central concept in rigorous analysis. In fact, at that stage, continuity is less a definition than a drawing rule: if your curve has no visible breaks, jumps, or sudden teleportations, then it is declared continuous. No *limits*, no *$\\epsilon$–$\\delta$*, just a pen and a steady hand.\n",
        "\n",
        "import plotly.graph_objects as go\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "x = np.linspace(-5, 5, 300)\n",
        "y = x**2\n",
        "\n",
        "fig = go.Figure()\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=x, y=y, mode=\"lines\"))\n",
        "fig.show()"
